comments.body:
@djbrooke to be frank: we shouldn't be sending information about our users to third parties without their consent—whether we are subject to GDPR or not. It's just the right thing to do!

As for a solution: I am not well versed in the Dataverse ecosystem, though I don't think we should need any custom code. We only need to replace the explicit call to Google Analytics to one that asks the user before setting cookies or sending hits. The [solution I linked](https://github.com/chiiya/haven/) is JavaScript and you literally just need to include it. Does Dataverse make site configuration available programmatically? If so, then you can template the JavaScript initiation to substitute the analytics ID, popup message, link etc that the site admin would configure in settings.

P.S. the popup at aussdata.at is a bad example in my opinion because it uses jargon, dark patterns, and is too complex. We should be using _very_ simple language with an easy option to reject. Do you remember how GitHub did it for a few days in 2020-12, which only lasted a few days until they [abandoned the popup altogether](https://github.blog/2020-12-17-no-cookie-for-you/)? They said "We are going to track you. Is that OK? Yes / No." Here's a good example on our [academic repository](https://cgspace.cgiar.org/). It really doesn't have to be complicated...
